 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Iowa&#x27;s explosive showdown 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Debris flies skyward this morning as the Army destroys degraded explosives at the Iowa Army Ammunition Plant in Middletown, Iowa. Photo: Scott Olson/Getty Images 🪖 The Army detonated 68 drums of unstable lead azide at an Iowa plant today after multiple delays, a court challenge and intervention from President Trump, Axios' Avery Lotz and Julianna Bragg write .

 Federal officials have repeatedly stressed that detonating the volatile material was safer than risking a spontaneous explosion. 💥 Two explosions erupted simultaneously in Middletown, Iowa.

 Large clouds of smoke shot into the air following the detonation, according to reporters at the scene. 🫡 Trump wrote on Truth Social : "Early reports from Iowa are that the Army's mitigation efforts have far exceeded expectations."

 Acting Secretary of the Army Adam Telle wrote on X : "The Army's controlled, mitigated detonation exceeded all expectations and was a HUGE success. The threat ... has been eliminated." Missouri Gov. Mike Kehoe (R) says from the neighboring state that the detonation was "well-contained," based on early analysis. 💣 Threat level:  The explosion raised concerns about exposing residents to lead, for which there are no established safe levels of exposure.

 Before the blast, officials said they didn't expect the release of the hazardous compounds. 🚧 The evacuation zone was a 5-mile cone extending southwest of the ammunition plant. 

